# project-rule

### Project Coding Standards and Architecture Guidelines

## For AI assistants

When **planning** or **implementing** any feature, treat this file as the **canonical** project-wide standard. Respect **layer boundaries** and **dependency rules** in **Clean Architecture** and **Layer dependency rules** below. **Concrete** tooling (async model, Result/Either type, DI library, JSON serializers, linters) is defined in **`.claude/rules/project-rule_[TECH]_[LANGUAGE].mdc`** when present; that file must stay aligned with every major topic here (see **Stack-specific project rules** at the end). When **authoring** or **regenerating** that tech-specific file (e.g. via `/cook`), use **`.claude/rules/templates/README.md`** and the matching **`.claude/rules/templates/*-project-rule.template.mdc`** (pick **Small / Medium / Big** tier) as **scaffold**, then ensure **full parity** with this common file. Prefer matching existing repo patterns over introducing new ones without cause.

### Mandatory pre-implementation discovery (requirements + UI/UX)

- Before implementing **any** feature (even “small” ones), first collect and review **all available inputs**: requirements/spec, existing project docs, existing screens, design reference images, Figma/design system docs, and the repo’s UI/UX reference materials.
- Do not start building new screens/components until you understand the project’s **existing design system/tokens**, component conventions, and reusable primitives.

### Prefer project-common building blocks first

- Before new libraries, ad-hoc helpers, duplicate UI, or alternate patterns, **look for what the project already centralizes** (e.g. `core/`, shared packages, design system, internal utilities, wrappers, config, error types). **Include shared presentation:** base **widgets** / components, **bottom sheets** and modals, **banners** and alerts, dialogs, sheets, toasts or snackbars, list cells, app bars or headers, empty and error states, and any other **common views** the repo already exposes.
- If nothing reusable exists, **introduce or extend the canonical shared place** so the capability is **one obvious home** for later features; avoid scattering near-identical copies across modules (including one-off bottom sheets, banners, or widgets that match an existing pattern).
- **Use those shared solutions by default** on every change. **Depart from them only when the user explicitly directs** a different library, pattern, or approach (still respecting layer and architecture rules).

### Shared UI kit bootstrap (do this early)

- When starting a new product area/app, create the **common reusable presentation primitives early** (then reuse them everywhere): button, text/typography, inputs, tabs, page view, indicators/loading, dialog, toast/snackbar, banner/alert, bottom sheet, list cells/items, section headers, empty/error states, etc.
- Prefer adding these to the repo’s **single canonical shared location** (design system / shared UI / core presentation) rather than creating per-feature one-offs.

